# 0016 — the run row says which model SERVED the run (servedModel)
# Why: a run row's `model` is the model the run ASKED for. The Claude CLI accepts a Fable request on an account without
# Fable and serves another model (claude-cli.js MODELS note), so model rankings built on `model` can credit Fable with
# another model's work. Every provider's own response names the model it served; StarNet read none of them. With this
# record each adapter reads it and puts it on its usage / done events as servedModel:
#   - claude-cli: the stream-json lines (the API's message_start and assistant message.model, which outrank the result
#     line's modelUsage, keyed by every model the turn used, helper models included: the one with the most output wins,
#     which outranks the system/init model the session was set to); persistent children keep their init model per child;
#   - anthropic: message_start message.model;   - openai-compatible and openrouter: a chunk's `model`.
# The loop keeps the last served model of the run and every distinct one when it changed mid-run (a failover, a router);
# the run row in runs.jsonl gains servedModel (string) and servedModels (array, only when more than one). A response that
# names no model adds nothing: such a row's model is "claimed" (unverified) to a reader. Additive: every other row and
# event is byte-identical. Test: test/served-model.test.js (a Fable request served by Fable, one served by another model,
# one that names none; each adapter, the loop and the row).
# Apply:  git -C starnet apply --3way company/patches/0016-served-model.diff ; then restart the station.
# Revert: git -C starnet apply -R company/patches/0016-served-model.diff ; then restart the station (rows already written
#         keep their servedModel field; readers treat a row without it as claimed).
